  "what_happened_next": {
    "text": "Microsoft led this set, with the international basket close behind. PepsiCo gained, but Coca-Cola lost about one-quarter of its value. The US basket finished slightly behind cash. Two companies selling similar products did not give investors the same result, and a crowded news month did not supply a clear answer.\n\nIn November 2001, Microsoft and the US government proposed a settlement of their competition case. The legal story had moved forward, though review was still required.\n\nEuro notes and coins began circulating in 2002. The Columbia shuttle was lost in 2003, and Hurricane Katrina devastated Gulf Coast communities in 2005. Scientific work, everyday business and terrible disasters all formed part of these years. No single headline captured everything investors would face.\n\nCoca-Cola and PepsiCo are an important contrast. Both sold familiar drinks, yet their shares did not deliver the same result. Buying a product often is different from knowing what its company’s stock will be worth later. The international basket offered another mix of businesses and currencies. Compare those choices with your own reasons at the start: which reasons were sensible, and which depended on a future you could not actually see?",
